course: english
lesson: 34
title: Apostrophes: missing letters and belonging
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An apostrophe is a little raised comma with two jobs: it marks letters that have been squeezed out ("do not" becomes "don't") and it shows that something belongs to someone ("the dog's bone").
explain: For missing letters, the apostrophe sits exactly where the letters vanished: in "can't" it stands for the missing "no" of "cannot", like a sticky note saying "something was here".
explain: For belonging, one owner takes 's (one girl's coat), many owners ending in s take just an apostrophe after the s (the girls' coats), and plurals without an s add 's (the children's toys).
explain: Apostrophes never make ordinary plurals: a market sign saying "Banana's £1" makes people giggle, because it should simply be "Bananas £1".
explain: The trickiest pair is its and it's: say "it is" in your head, and if that fits, write "it's"; a Londoner's "It's bare cold" means "It is very cold", while "The dog wagged its tail" has no apostrophe at all.
fact: An apostrophe can show missing letters in a contraction: don't (do not), can't (cannot), I'm (I am), we're (we are).
fact: The apostrophe goes exactly where the letters are missing, not where the words used to meet.
fact: For one owner, add 's: the dog's bone, Sam's bike.
fact: For a plural owner that already ends in s, add only an apostrophe: the girls' coats, the teachers' room.
fact: For a plural that does not end in s, add 's: the children's toys, the men's changing room.
fact: Ordinary plurals never take an apostrophe: bananas, apples, cars.
fact: Its (no apostrophe) means belonging to it; it's means it is or it has.
fact: Whose shows belonging ("Whose coat is this?"); who's means who is or who has.
fact: For names ending in s, both James's book and James' book are accepted; many schools teach James's.
fact: Your means belonging to you; you're means you are. Were is a past verb; we're means we are.
fact: In Scotland a "wean" is a child; apostrophe rules work the same with any noun: "the wean's ball".
fact: O'clock is short for "of the clock", so its apostrophe also marks missing letters.
exercise: Ben writes "dont". How should it be written? | don't | do'nt | dont' | 'dont
exercise: Why does the apostrophe go between n and t in "don't"? | That is where the "o" of "not" is missing | That is where "do" and "not" used to meet | Apostrophes always go before the last letter | It looks neater in the middle of the word
exercise: Teacher writes "The dog's bone." Leo asks "So the bone belongs to the dog?" What is the answer? | Yes, the 's shows that one dog owns it | No, it is short for "the dog is bone" | No, it means there are many dogs | Yes, but only if the bone is big
exercise: Ava asks why "The girls' coats" has the apostrophe after the s. What is the reason? | There are many girls, and the plural already ends in s | Apostrophes always go at the very end of a word | There is only one girl with several coats | The s was added later by mistake
exercise: Grandad in Glasgow says "That's the wean's ball." Is the apostrophe in "wean's" correct? | Yes, one child owns the ball | No, dialect words never take apostrophes | No, it should be "weans' ball" for one child | Yes, because wean is a plural
exercise: A shop sign says "Banana's £1". Why does Mum laugh? | It is a plain plural, so it needs no apostrophe | The price is far too cheap for bananas | It should say "Bananas's £1" | Bananas should have a capital letter
exercise: Which is correct? | the children's toys | the childrens' toys | the childrens toy's | the children's' toys
exercise: Kai asks "James's book or James' book?" What is the best answer? | Both are accepted; follow your school's rule | Only James' is right, because of the s | Only James's is right, all over the world | Neither, it should be "Jame's book"
exercise: Mum in London texts "Its bare cold, take ur coat." Which should it be? | It's, because it means "it is" | Its, because it shows belonging | It's, because cold belongs to it | Its', because of the s
exercise: Year 6: "The cats tail was long." There is one cat. Which word needs an apostrophe? | cats | tail | long | was
exercise: Josh, Gen Z, says "I'm gonna skip apostrophes, they're cringe." Why would that cause trouble? | "Were" and "we're" mean different things | Apostrophes are only for names, so nothing changes | Spellcheck always adds them back anyway | It would make the words much longer
exercise: Which sentence uses "its" correctly? | The dog wagged its tail. | The dog wagged it's tail. | Its raining again. | Its' going to snow.
exercise: Which is correct for "who is"? | who's | whose | who'se | whos
exercise: Which sentence uses your and you're correctly? | You're late for your swimming lesson. | Your late for you're swimming lesson. | Your late for your swimming lesson. | You're late for you're swimming lesson.
exercise: Why does o'clock have an apostrophe? | It is short for "of the clock" | Clocks belong to somebody | It stops "oclock" rhyming with "sock" | The o was added to sound fancy
exercise: Which shows the coats of many teachers? | the teachers' coats | the teacher's coats | the teachers coat's | the teacher coats'
test: Your mate says "apostrophes just mean there's an s coming, innit." What is wrong with that? | Many plurals with s need none, and some apostrophes have no s | Nothing, that is exactly the rule | Apostrophes only ever come before an s | It only goes wrong in American English
test: ★★★ "The girl's dogs' bowls." How many girls and how many dogs? | One girl and more than one dog | Many girls and one dog | One girl and one dog | Many girls and many dogs
test: In a cartoon, a sign says "Free kitten's to good home." What should it say? | Free kittens to good home | Free kittens' to good home | Free kitten's' to good home | Free kitten to good home's
test: A lad in Cork says "Whose round is it, yer man's?" ("Yer man" means that fella there.) Why "whose", not "who's"? | It asks about belonging, not "who is" | Whose is the short form of who is | Who's is only used in questions | They are the same word, just spelled two ways
test: What would happen if you wrote "We were here" as "We're here"? | It would change from the past to the present | Nothing at all, the meaning stays exactly the same | It would become a question | It would show belonging
test: Dad, bit snappy: "It's not 'your welcome', it's 'you're welcome', get it right." Why is Dad right? | "You're" stands for "you are" | "Your" is always a rude word | "You're" shows belonging | "Welcome" needs an apostrophe
test: ★★★ How many apostrophes are needed in "Its the mens football match and its kick-off is at six oclock"? | Three | Two | Four | Five
test: Aussie kid: "Me mates footy boots are heaps muddy." ("Footy" is football, "heaps" means very.) One mate. Where is the apostrophe in standard writing? | mate's | mates' | boot's | muddy's
test: Which shows the toys belong to the women? | the women's toys | the womens' toys | the womans' toys | the women toy's
test: Cousin in Ohio, Gen Alpha: "This sandwich is bussin', its fire." ("Bussin'" and "fire" both mean really good.) What should "its" be? | it's | its' | it is' | its's
test: ★★★ "The horses' stables" and "the horse's stables". In which do the stables belong to more than one horse? | The horses' stables | The horse's stables | Both of them | Neither of them
exam: Identify the correctly punctuated form denoting the toys belonging to a group of children. | the children's toys | the childrens' toys | the childrens toys' | the children'ss toys
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of the apostrophe in "can't"? | It marks the omission of letters | It denotes possession | It indicates a plural | It separates two clauses
exam: Select the sentence consistent with the established rules concerning "its" and "it's". | It's late and the cat wants its tea. | Its late and the cat wants it's tea. | It's late and the cat wants it's tea. | Its late and the cat wants its tea.
exam: Ascertain the correct form signifying that a bone is in the possession of a single dog. | the dog's bone | the dogs' bone | the dogs bone | the dog bone's
